      "pronunciationText": "Now the points of inflection. Differentiate again with the product rule, and factorise. Again f of x is positive, so the sign of the second derivative comes from this bracket. It's zero when the quantity x minus mu, squared, equals sigma squared, so at mu plus or minus sigma. Between those points the bracket is negative, so the curve is concave down, like a cap over the peak. Outside them it's concave up, and the tails flatten out. The concavity changes, so these are points of inflection, at one thirty-five and one sixty-five. So one sigma is where the bell changes its bend."
